INT. NIGHT-SHIFT DINER - BOOTH - 3 A.M.

Vinyl booths. A flickering OPEN sign. Rain on the glass. A jukebox plays something low.

RUTH ALMEIDA-STONE, forties, sits with her coat on and a cold coffee. Across from her, her sister LORELEI, thirties, stares into her own cup. A WAITRESS stands at the end of the booth with a pot, patient.

Nobody speaks. Ruth turns the sugar dispenser in a slow circle.

RUTH
You came all this way.

LORELEI
I said I would.

WAITRESS
Refill, ladies?

RUTH
Please.

The waitress pours. Lorelei puts her hand over her cup, then removes it, then lets the waitress pour.

A bell over the door jingles. A MAN in a soaked trench coat steps in, scans the room, and heads toward their booth.

MAN
(low)
Ruth.

Ruth does not look up. Lorelei does.

LORELEI
Who is this?

RUTH
Nobody.

MAN
I'm not nobody. I'm the one who drove four hours.

He sits next to Lorelei without being invited. She slides away, but he's already too close.

LORELEI
Excuse me?

MAN
Ruth, tell her.

RUTH
Don't.

MAN
Tell her about Dad.

Lorelei turns to her sister.

LORELEI
What about Dad?

Ruth says nothing. The waitress retreats. Through the kitchen pass, a COOK in a stained apron slaps a bell.

COOK (O.S.)
Order up! The jukebox ends one song and begins another.

MAN
She hasn't told you.

LORELEI
Ruth.

RUTH
He's not who you think he was.

The man leans back, satisfied, and slips off his hat. Under the diner light, he has their father's chin.

LORELEI
(whispering)
Who are you?

BERNARD
Bernard Almeida-Stone. Your brother.

Lorelei stares at him. Then at Ruth. Ruth finally looks up, and she is crying, and not hiding it.

RUTH
I wanted to tell you. I swear I did.

Lorelei slides out of the booth and stands. She picks up her purse. Bernard stands too. Ruth reaches for her sister's sleeve, and Lorelei steps back.

LORELEI
Sit down.

Ruth sits. Bernard sits. Lorelei does not.
